  "narrative": "Final synthesis at this checkpoint focuses on the two due validation results and then places them within the broader evidence set.\n\nH99 is accepted and closed. The hypothesis is that CDKN2A loss is associated with more negative MDM2 dependency score, i.e. stronger MDM2 dependency, among TP53-intact cells. Because the claim direction is -1, the positive signed estimates mean the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is negative. Discovery R54 estimated +0.1185 [0.0793, 0.1577]; independent validation R66 estimated +0.1162 [0.0584, 0.1741]. These are highly concordant in magnitude and precision. Relative to the average absolute MDM2 dependency score, the validation lower bound is far above the 10% practical benchmark, and the point estimate is very large in dependency-score units. This is one of the most robust dataset-specific associations discovered: CDKN2A-loss cells show stronger MDM2 dependency, with consistency across TP53-intact, TP53-loss, MTAP-intact, and PTEN-intact contexts.\n\nH116 is accepted and closed. The hypothesis is that PTEN loss is associated with more negative STAT3 dependency score, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency. With direction -1, the positive signed estimates indicate the raw contrast is in the hypothesized direction. Discovery R78 estimated +0.2674 [0.2153, 0.3195]; independent validation R93 estimated +0.2770 [0.1967, 0.3572]. The validation lower bound remains far above 10% of the average absolute STAT3 dependency score, and the effect is stable in TP53-intact, PIK3CA-wild-type, and adenocarcinoma contexts. I therefore treat PTEN loss as a strongly supported predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency within this supplied dataset, without implying causality or external generalizability.\n\nSupported dataset-specific findings now center on five biologically coherent axes: SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, robust across TP53-intact and TP53-loss strata and several context subsets; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency, independently validated and robust across p53/MTAP/PTEN contexts; TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, independently validated and robust in the dominant library and MTAP-intact strata; and PTEN loss with much stronger STAT3 dependency, now independently validated and robust across TP53-intact, PIK3CA-wild-type, and adenocarcinoma contexts.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings include: no support for the original hypothesis that NF1 loss strengthens KRAS dependency among KRAS-wild-type cells, and no support that NF1 loss strengthens RIT1 dependency in the same setting; no support that MYC amplification is a simple predictor of stronger POLR2A, RPL11, DHX9, or USP7 dependency; no meaningful association of the arbitrary research markers D/E/F with SMARCA2, WRN, or NFE2L2 dependency; and little evidence that technical screen-quality or batch-like variables strongly moderate the largest accepted biological associations, although some interaction intervals remain wide.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains. The opposite-direction NF1-loss effects on KRAS dependency among KRAS-wild-type cells (H111/H123) have positive intervals but lower bounds below the practical 10% benchmark and are post-hoc relative to the original hypotheses, so they are deferred rather than accepted. The opposite-direction TP53-loss association with DHX9 dependency (H148) is similarly small and clinically uncertain. EGFR amplification in PIK3CA-wild-type cells (H122) shows an opposite-direction near-threshold signal, and the CDKN2A-loss/TP53-loss interaction for MDM2 (H124) and USP7 TP53-loss/CDKN2A-loss interaction (H131) are too imprecise to conclude effect modification. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset only.\n\nThe most informative next analyses should refine and localize the accepted findings rather than repeatedly retest their overall existence. For SMARCA2, test whether ARID1A co-loss enhances the SMARCA4-loss effect. For USP7, stratify the TP53-loss effect by CDKN2A-loss status. For STAT3, test PTEN-loss effects and interactions within PIK3CA-mutant biology and TP53-loss strata. For NFE2L2, extend the KEAP1-loss association into MTAP-loss strata. For pathway-driver dependencies, test EGFR and ERBB2 dependency among KRAS/BRAF-wild-type cells and KRAS dependency among BRAF/EGFR-wild-type cells to determine whether apparent null effects are masked by co-occurring driver pathways. Independent validation remains valuable only for newly accepted strong effects or sharply refined context hypotheses; low-priority small opposite-direction effects should not consume validation budget.",
  "research_notes": "After validation of H99 and H116, accepted dataset-specific core is: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, robust across TP53 contexts; CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency, validated and robust across TP53, MTAP, PTEN contexts; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency, validated and robust in Avana and MTAP-intact strata; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency, validated and robust overall, TP53-intact, PIK3CA-WT, and adenocarcinoma contexts. Negative/unsupported: original NF1/KRAS stronger-dependency hypothesis, NF1/RIT1 hypothesis, MYC-amplification dependencies, arbitrary research markers D/E/F, many technical moderation interactions. Unresolved deferred: opposite-direction NF1/KRAS effects in KRAS-wt (H111/H123), opposite TP53/DHX9 effect (H148), EGFR-ampl in PIK3CA-WT opposite near-threshold (H122), CDKN2A/TP53 MDM2 interaction (H124), USP7 TP53/CDKN2A interaction (H131). Next analyze should prioritize newly registered context/refinement proposals plus existing registered tests: SMARCA4-loss interaction with ARID1A-loss; USP7 TP53-loss stratified by CDKN2A-loss; PTEN/STAT3 in PIK3CA-mutant and TP53-loss interaction; KEAP1/NFE2L2 in MTAP-loss; EGFR and ERBB2 dependency in KRAS/BRAF-wt; KRAS dependency in BRAF/EGFR-wt; and selected existing driver-context claims. Validation budget should be reserved for strong new context acceptances or sharp reversal/interaction hypotheses, not small post-hoc lower-bound-below-threshold effects. All claims are associations within supplied dataset, not causal or external generalizability claims."
