  "narrative": "Due validation assessment. H118 is accepted and closed: TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency. The original discovery contrast R71 was +0.396 [0.325, 0.467], and independent validation R79 is +0.380 [0.269, 0.491]. Because the hypothesis direction is +1, these signed estimates mean the raw TP53-loss exposed-minus-comparator contrast is positive, i.e. TP53-loss cell lines have less negative USP7 dependency scores. The magnitude is many-fold larger than a practical 10%-of-average-absolute-score benchmark for dependency_USP7, and discovery and validation are highly concordant despite multiplicity-adjusted validation intervals. This is now a robustly supported dataset-specific association. H133 is accepted and closed: PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency among TP53-loss cell lines. Discovery R99 was +0.190 [0.085, 0.296] and independent validation R106 is +0.243 [0.087, 0.400]. With direction -1, the raw contrast is negative, so PTEN-null lines have more negative STAT3 dependency scores. Although this TP53-loss stratum is smaller and somewhat less precise than the overall PTEN effect, the lower bound remains clearly above the practical clinical-significance benchmark, so I accept it as a dataset-specific subgroup-supported finding without claiming causality. H109 remains rejected and closed: there is no support for a clinically meaningful interaction in which MSI-high cells have stronger WRN dependency specifically in high screen_roc_auc strata. Discovery R64 was essentially null and opposite to the hypothesized direction, and validation R80 is also small and opposite (-0.012 [-0.105, 0.082]), crossing zero. The interval is wide enough that exact absence cannot be proven, but there is no positive evidence, so further resources are not warranted. H131 is left unresolved but closed: whether the TP53-loss association with weaker USP7 dependency is modified by CDKN2A-loss status is not established. Discovery R86 was tiny, and validation R107 is -0.065 [-0.312, 0.182], very wide and opposite to the tested interaction direction. This neither confirms nor excludes effect modification with sufficient precision, but the tested hypothesis is not supported and should not consume further validation or focused analysis unless a new mechanistic rationale emerges. Overall, the supplied dataset now supports a coherent set of measured associations: SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency; TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency; and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. Several of these are robust across TP53/MTAP/CDKN2A/PIK3CA/adenocarcinoma/library/culture contexts already tested. Negative or unsupported findings include the original hypotheses that ERBB2 amplification predicts stronger ERBB2 dependency, NF1 loss predicts stronger KRAS or RIT1 dependency among KRAS-wild-type cells, MYC amplification is a simple predictor of POLR2A/RPL11/DHX9/USP7 dependency, or arbitrary research markers D/E/F predict SMARCA2, WRN, or NFE2L2 dependency. EGFR amplification as a predictor of TMED10 dependency and RB1 loss as a predictor of KIF18A dependency remain near null or underpowered. Unresolved uncertainty remains around some near-threshold opposite-direction or driver-stratified contrasts, including EGFR amplification in PIK3CA-wild-type cells, weak opposite-direction NF1-loss effects on KRAS dependency, small TP53-loss associations with DHX9, and several moderator interactions with wide intervals. The next most informative analyses are therefore localization and robustness rather than repeated overall testing: culture-type and serum strata for the accepted axes, and driver-null stratified tests of EGFR, ERBB2, TMED10, and KIF18A dependencies. Remaining validation capacity should be reserved for newly accepted high-magnitude associations, not for near-null post-hoc